39,2,1,talk,May,,"Well, the archives were quite revealing! It turns out some of our ""innovations"" actually worked—like the contactless ferry trial and late-evening timetables. However, we did fail to maintain some lane-priority stretches. We must be careful not to repeat that.","{""words"":39,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
40,2,1,talk,Benjamin,,"My findings are rather sobering. It appears we are operating on a skeleton crew; the late-turn pool is depleted, and our roadway and signals teams are significantly understaffed. We are essentially running on overtime and hope.","{""words"":36,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
41,2,1,talk,Midori,,"That is worrying, @Benjamin. My findings show that passengers are losing most of their time waiting for connections, not in queues. Interestingly, mid-city trips take longer now, even though the crowds aren't actually larger.","{""words"":34,""mentions"":[""Benjamin""],""raw_differs"":false}"
42,2,1,talk,Shizue,,"It is a bit concerning, isn't it? I found that work in the old station shells is very risky due to hidden voids, and getting ferry and rail offices to agree is quite difficult. We must be very careful.","{""words"":39,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
43,2,1,talk,Jonathan,,"My findings are equally concerning. We are paying excessive fees to multiple ticketing contractors, and paving costs are rising. Meanwhile, our operating subsidies are being trimmed, leaving us with a precarious financial foundation.","{""words"":33,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
44,2,2,talk,May,,"@Benjamin, it sounds like we're in a bit of a crisis! If we're running on ""hope,"" we definitely can't afford a flashy new project that ignores the staffing reality or fails like those lane-priority stretches did.","{""words"":36,""mentions"":[""Benjamin""],""raw_differs"":false}"
45,2,2,talk,Benjamin,,"@May, ""crisis"" is a strong word, though perhaps an accurate one. Logic suggests that any plan requiring additional operational intensity will simply collapse under its own weight if we don't address the staffing void first.","{""words"":35,""mentions"":[""May""],""raw_differs"":false}"
46,2,2,talk,Midori,,"@Benjamin, I agree. If we focus on the wrong areas, we might waste those few resources we have. We should prioritize fixing the connection delays, as that's where the real time-loss is happening for passengers.","{""words"":35,""mentions"":[""Benjamin""],""raw_differs"":false}"
47,2,2,talk,Shizue,,"@Midori, you are so right. But we must also be mindful that some paths are harder to walk than others. If a plan requires too many approvals or risks hidden surprises, it may never truly begin.","{""words"":36,""mentions"":[""Midori""],""raw_differs"":false}"
48,2,2,talk,Jonathan,,"I agree with @Shizue. We must be cautious. If we commit to a plan with high upfront costs or complex contracts, we risk further draining our subsidies while our fixed fees to contractors continue to climb.","{""words"":36,""mentions"":[""Shizue""],""raw_differs"":false}"
49,2,3,talk,May,,"It's almost funny how we always forget the basics! We have proven successes in contactless payments and evening schedules, yet we're staring at a staffing void and budget leaks. Why reinvent the wheel when we have blueprints that actually worked?","{""words"":40,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
50,2,3,talk,Benjamin,,"@May, your optimism is refreshing, but blueprints are useless if there is no one to hold the hammer. We cannot simply ""reuse"" a success if the crews required to maintain it have vanished into a recruitment freeze.","{""words"":37,""mentions"":[""May""],""raw_differs"":false}"
51,2,3,talk,Midori,,"I understand, @Benjamin. But if we only focus on the staff, we might miss the chance to simplify the journey. If we can reduce connection delays, we might actually ease some of the pressure on the system.","{""words"":37,""mentions"":[""Benjamin""],""raw_differs"":false}"
52,2,3,talk,Shizue,,"I understand your point, @Midori. But we must be gentle with our expectations. If we choose a plan that looks lovely on paper but requires agreements from offices that cannot agree, we will only cause more frustration for everyone.","{""words"":39,""mentions"":[""Midori""],""raw_differs"":false}"
53,2,3,talk,Jonathan,,"I agree. We must avoid any plan that creates new, long-term financial liabilities. With our subsidies shrinking and contractor fees rising, we cannot afford a strategy that relies on optimistic funding or expensive, rigid contracts.","{""words"":35,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
54,2,4,talk,May,,"Exactly! We need a plan that is lean, realistic, and based on what we know works. Let's avoid the ""grand vision"" trap and look for something that respects our budget and our exhausted staff.","{""words"":34,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
55,2,4,talk,Benjamin,,Precisely. A plan that ignores the human cost is not a strategy; it is a fantasy. I shall support any option that prioritizes stability and operational feasibility over mere aesthetic or theoretical improvement.,"{""words"":33,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
56,2,4,talk,Midori,,"I hope we can find a balance. Let's look for a plan that targets those connection delays to help the passengers, but does so in a way that doesn't overwhelm our remaining staff or budget.","{""words"":35,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
57,2,4,talk,Shizue,,I agree. Let us look for the path of least resistance. A plan that avoids the old station shells and doesn't require impossible agreements will be the kindest choice for everyone.,"{""words"":31,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
58,2,4,talk,Jonathan,,I concur. We must prioritize a plan that is fiscally sustainable and avoids adding to our contractor burdens. Let us seek a solution that provides genuine value without compromising our long-term financial stability.,"{""words"":33,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
