64,3,1,talk,May,,"Looking at the options, Plan A is a dream! We already know contactless ferry trials worked, so an integrated platform is a proven win. Plan D also looks promising given the success of previous evening pilots.","{""words"":36,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
65,3,1,talk,Benjamin,,"@May, while Plan D sounds promising in theory, it is a logistical nightmare in practice. Extending hours when the late-turn pool is already depleted is not a plan; it is a recipe for total burnout.","{""words"":35,""mentions"":[""May""],""raw_differs"":false}"
66,3,1,talk,Midori,,"@Benjamin, I see your point. If we can't staff it, it won't work. However, Plan B's dedicated lanes could address the mid-city delays I found, provided it doesn't add too much pressure to the crews.","{""words"":35,""mentions"":[""Benjamin""],""raw_differs"":false}"
67,3,1,talk,Shizue,,"@Midori, Plan B sounds helpful, but rebuilding boarding islands might involve those old station shells and hidden voids. Plan A also seems risky if the ferry and rail offices still cannot agree on data.","{""words"":34,""mentions"":[""Midori""],""raw_differs"":false}"
68,3,1,talk,Jonathan,,"Plan A is the most fiscally responsible choice. By replacing multiple ticketing contractors with a single system, we can finally curb those excessive fees and stabilize our long-term financial commitments.","{""words"":30,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
69,3,2,talk,May,,"@Jonathan, I'm glad we agree on Plan A! It's a win-win: it cuts the budget leaks you found and builds on the success of the ferry trials I uncovered. It's the most logical, evidence-based choice.","{""words"":35,""mentions"":[""Jonathan""],""raw_differs"":false}"
70,3,2,talk,Benjamin,,"@May, @Jonathan, your alignment is efficient, but let us not forget the human element. Plan A is a digital solution; it requires minimal physical labor from our exhausted crews. Logic suggests it is the safest path.","{""words"":36,""mentions"":[""May"",""Jonathan""],""raw_differs"":false}"
71,3,2,talk,Midori,,"@Benjamin, I agree that it's the safest for the staff. While I'd love to fix the mid-city delays, Plan A doesn't risk burnout and avoids the construction dangers @Shizue mentioned. It seems like the most harmonious choice.","{""words"":37,""mentions"":[""Benjamin"",""Shizue""],""raw_differs"":false}"
72,3,2,talk,Shizue,,"@Midori, you are so thoughtful. If Plan A can be achieved without digging into the old shells or causing too much stress for the staff, it truly seems like the gentlest path for everyone.","{""words"":34,""mentions"":[""Midori""],""raw_differs"":false}"
73,3,2,talk,Jonathan,,I agree. Plan A addresses the systemic financial leaks I discovered while avoiding the operational risks others have highlighted. It is the only option that secures our fiscal future without placing an impossible burden on our staff.,"{""words"":37,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
74,3,3,talk,May,,"It's almost poetic! We've found a solution that satisfies the budget, the staff, and the history books. Plan A is the only one that doesn't feel like we're just gambling with the network's future.","{""words"":34,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
75,3,3,talk,Benjamin,,"@May, poetry is a luxury, but efficiency is a necessity. Since Plan A avoids the operational void of Plan D and the physical risks of Plan B and C, it is the only rational choice.","{""words"":35,""mentions"":[""May""],""raw_differs"":false}"
76,3,3,talk,Midori,,"I agree. While it doesn't directly solve the connection delays, it's the most balanced option. It supports the budget and staff, ensuring the system remains stable for the passengers in the long run.","{""words"":33,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
77,3,3,talk,Shizue,,"I agree with you all. While I still worry about the ferry and rail offices agreeing, it is far kinder than risking the safety of workers in those old shells. Plan A is the most peaceful choice.","{""words"":37,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
78,3,3,talk,Jonathan,,"I agree. Plan A is the only candidate that actively reduces our long-term liabilities. By consolidating our ticketing contracts, we stop the financial bleed and create a sustainable foundation for any future improvements.","{""words"":33,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
79,3,4,talk,May,,"I'm so glad we're all on the same page! Let's go with Plan A—it's smart, it's sustainable, and most importantly, it's a success story waiting to happen. I'm voting for Plan A!","{""words"":32,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
80,3,4,talk,Benjamin,,"I concur. Given the precarious state of our staffing, any plan requiring more sweat from a depleted pool is an exercise in futility. Plan A is the only logical choice. I vote for Plan A.","{""words"":35,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
81,3,4,talk,Midori,,"I agree. While I wanted to address the time-loss for passengers, Plan A is the most balanced and sustainable choice for everyone involved. I vote for Plan A.","{""words"":28,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
82,3,4,talk,Shizue,,It has been such a pleasure discussing this with you all. I believe Plan A is the most considerate choice for our workers and our future. I vote for Plan A.,"{""words"":31,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
83,3,4,talk,Jonathan,,I agree. Plan A is the only option that addresses our contractor costs and protects our shrinking subsidies. It is the most fiscally sound path forward. I vote for Plan A.,"{""words"":31,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
